    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Thus, under a backcross design the scaled QTL effects are influenced strongly by the (unknown) degree of dominance k. If Q is completely dominant to q, k = 1, and there is no marker-QTL effect. Conversely, if q is dominant to Q, k = -1 and the scaled effect becomes $ 2a(1 - 2c) $, which is the same as under an $ F_2 $ design. Recalling Equation 15.3b, the reciprocal backcross ( $ B_2 = F_1 \\times P_2 $) yields a similar expression,",
      "source_page": 452,
      "printed_page": 432,
