    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "The expressions developed in Example 2 for $ F_2 $ analysis hold for an $ F_t $ population, provided $ \\widetilde{c} $ (given by Equation 15.4) replaces $ c $. For example, $ \\mu_{MM} - \\mu_{mm} = 2a(1 - 2\\widetilde{c}) $, and so forth. Since $ (1 - 2c) > (1 - 2\\widetilde{c}) $, AILs have smaller differences between marker means, and hence reduced power of QTL detection, relative to the $ F_2 $ design. Despite this, Darvasi and Soller (1995) advocate the use of AILs for fine-mapping of QTLs, as the expansion of the genetic map offers a higher precision of estimates of QTL position. We expand on this point below.",
      "source_page": 452,
      "printed_page": 432,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter15:p008:b15"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        138.0,
